I MOM2 from us would not have multiplayer. But an expansion pack or MOM3
from us would probably have it.  We want to concentrate heavily on having a
good AI in this and multiplayer would require us to make sacrifices
somewhere.

Instead, I would like to see a virtual "kingdom" created where spells,
artifacts, and other things can be randomly found in game and traded on-line
and used as well as a Metaverse type component where extra goodies get
unlocked based on the size of your empire and such.

Anyway, my point about MOO and GalCiv is this: Of course GalCiv isn't that
much like MOO because any similarities between the two are as coincidental
as the similarities between it and Grand Theft Auto.   But if you're making
a sequel to an existing game you are (IMO) supposed to be trying to take the
best elements of the original and expand on them.

I picture a MOM 2 being essentially MOM 1 with new graphics (on a 3D engine
so you can do all kinds of cool spell effects), more sophisticated AI, a
campaign, and some more city building features.

But we're still taking quite a ways in the future.  We hope to begin
development of it next Spring but that depends on staffing (getting good 3D
modelers in Michigan has been..challenging and we're going to need some
incredibly good 3D artists for this).

